1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:01,910
Walk into Gardens by the Bay.

2
00:00:02,100 --> 00:00:04,150
Look up at the giant Supertrees.

3
00:00:04,330 --> 00:00:06,510
And ask yourself one strange question:

4
00:00:06,910 --> 00:00:08,630
what was standing right here,

5
00:00:08,740 --> 00:00:09,630
sixty years ago?

6
00:00:10,320 --> 00:00:11,920
Nothing. It was the sea.

7
00:00:12,320 --> 00:00:13,830
The park covers one hundred and one

8
00:00:13,850 --> 00:00:15,300
hectares, and every one of them is

9
00:00:15,320 --> 00:00:16,190
reclaimed land.

10
00:00:16,530 --> 00:00:17,770
That's the first thing about Singapore

11
00:00:17,790 --> 00:00:19,580
that most visitors never notice.

12
00:00:19,890 --> 00:00:21,740
A big part of what you came to see isn't

13
00:00:21,760 --> 00:00:22,600
original ground.

14
00:00:23,030 --> 00:00:24,620
In this video, you'll meet three things

15
00:00:24,640 --> 00:00:26,610
every visitor runs into without really

16
00:00:26,630 --> 00:00:27,470
understanding them.

17
00:00:27,880 --> 00:00:29,230
The ground that was made.

18
00:00:29,410 --> 00:00:30,910
The rule everybody jokes about.

19
00:00:31,190 --> 00:00:33,220
And a place just minutes away by boat that

20
00:00:33,240 --> 00:00:35,480
shows what Singapore chose not to become.

21
00:00:35,800 --> 00:00:37,350
That last one is where the real surprise

22
00:00:37,370 --> 00:00:39,230
is. Start with the ground.

23
00:00:39,610 --> 00:00:41,320
Marina Bay, the district behind that

24
00:00:41,340 --> 00:00:42,490
famous skyline, is a

25
00:00:42,510 --> 00:00:44,520
three-hundred-and-sixty-hectare extension

26
00:00:44,540 --> 00:00:45,540
of the city center.

27
00:00:45,950 --> 00:00:47,450
Work to create it began in nineteen

28
00:00:47,470 --> 00:00:49,050
sixty-nine, and wasn't finished until

29
00:00:49,070 --> 00:00:50,240
nineteen ninety-two.

30
00:00:50,680 --> 00:00:52,550
Marina Bay Sands and Gardens by the Bay

31
00:00:52,570 --> 00:00:53,810
both sit on top of it.

32
00:00:54,110 --> 00:00:55,640
And that's only one corner.

33
00:00:55,930 --> 00:00:57,890
Since nineteen sixty, Singapore has grown

34
00:00:57,910 --> 00:01:00,100
by roughly a quarter, from about five

35
00:01:00,120 --> 00:01:02,020
hundred eighty square kilometers to about

36
00:01:02,040 --> 00:01:03,320
seven hundred thirty-six.

37
00:01:03,610 --> 00:01:05,740
A country that added a quarter of itself,

38
00:01:05,820 --> 00:01:08,200
from water. There's a detail you can only

39
00:01:08,220 --> 00:01:09,450
see if you know where to look.

40
00:01:09,720 --> 00:01:11,490
In two thousand eight, the basin in front

41
00:01:11,510 --> 00:01:13,470
of all this was closed off by a barrage,

42
00:01:13,780 --> 00:01:15,500
and what used to be open bay became a

43
00:01:15,520 --> 00:01:16,780
freshwater reservoir.

44
00:01:17,160 --> 00:01:19,050
The view in your photo is a lake that used

45
00:01:19,070 --> 00:01:21,020
to be the sea. Keep that idea.

46
00:01:21,360 --> 00:01:23,510
Singapore doesn't just live on its land.

47
00:01:23,650 --> 00:01:25,880
It designs it. Now, the rule.

48
00:01:26,030 --> 00:01:26,660
You've heard it.

49
00:01:26,680 --> 00:01:28,170
Singapore banned chewing gum.

50
00:01:28,420 --> 00:01:30,050
So should you panic about the pack in your

51
00:01:30,070 --> 00:01:32,730
bag? Here's what actually happened.

52
00:01:32,750 --> 00:01:34,500
The ban was announced to start on January

53
00:01:34,520 --> 00:01:36,020
third, nineteen ninety-two.

54
00:01:36,270 --> 00:01:37,860
The reasons were very specific:

55
00:01:38,090 --> 00:01:39,850
gum stuck in subway door sensors,

56
00:01:39,870 --> 00:01:42,010
in lock cylinders, on elevator buttons.

57
00:01:42,210 --> 00:01:44,890
Practical, expensive, annoying problems.

58
00:01:45,150 --> 00:01:46,800
But here's what most travel posts get

59
00:01:46,820 --> 00:01:49,290
wrong. Gum isn't erased from the country.

60
00:01:49,520 --> 00:01:51,380
Since two thousand four, a narrow

61
00:01:51,400 --> 00:01:53,630
exception exists for therapeutic,

62
00:01:53,760 --> 00:01:56,070
dental and nicotine gum, which you can

63
00:01:56,090 --> 00:01:58,350
only get from a doctor or a registered

64
00:01:58,370 --> 00:02:00,570
pharmacist. And a pharmacist is allowed to

65
00:02:00,590 --> 00:02:02,050
ask you why you want it.

66
00:02:02,400 --> 00:02:03,990
That exception didn't come from a change

67
00:02:04,010 --> 00:02:06,200
of heart. It came with a trade deal,

68
00:02:06,360 --> 00:02:08,520
the US–Singapore Free Trade Agreement.

69
00:02:08,810 --> 00:02:10,840
And in twenty-ten, the government told

70
00:02:10,860 --> 00:02:12,820
parliament it was standing by the ban.

71
00:02:13,200 --> 00:02:14,750
So what does that tell you about the place

72
00:02:14,770 --> 00:02:15,370
you're visiting?

73
00:02:15,820 --> 00:02:17,940
That it's a city that solves a problem by

74
00:02:17,960 --> 00:02:20,020
writing a rule, and keeps the rule.

75
00:02:20,430 --> 00:02:22,290
Which brings us to the strangest stop on

76
00:02:22,310 --> 00:02:24,400
this list. Back on the main island,

77
00:02:24,420 --> 00:02:26,760
in a park built in nineteen thirty-seven

78
00:02:26,950 --> 00:02:28,760
by the brothers behind Tiger Balm,

79
00:02:28,780 --> 00:02:30,700
there's a place called Haw Par Villa.

80
00:02:31,100 --> 00:02:33,480
Inside, the Ten Courts of Hell:

81
00:02:33,750 --> 00:02:35,720
scenes of punishment, in brightly painted

82
00:02:35,740 --> 00:02:38,510
statues. Nothing in the glossy skyline you

83
00:02:38,530 --> 00:02:40,440
just left would lead you to expect it.

84
00:02:40,720 --> 00:02:42,810
It's the Singapore that no master plan

85
00:02:42,830 --> 00:02:45,560
produced. But for the real contrast,

86
00:02:45,580 --> 00:02:46,330
you need a boat.

87
00:02:46,450 --> 00:02:48,510
Go to the Changi Point Ferry Terminal,

88
00:02:48,530 --> 00:02:50,070
at the eastern end of the island.

89
00:02:50,240 --> 00:02:52,850
From there, small boats called bumboats

90
00:02:53,010 --> 00:02:54,440
cross to Pulau Ubin.

91
00:02:54,670 --> 00:02:56,190
The ride takes about ten minutes,

92
00:02:56,350 --> 00:02:58,680
and costs roughly four Singapore dollars.

93
00:02:58,890 --> 00:02:59,860
There's no timetable.

94
00:03:00,020 --> 00:03:01,330
The boat leaves when it's full,

95
00:03:01,370 --> 00:03:03,100
twelve people, or when you pay for the

96
00:03:03,120 --> 00:03:04,100
whole boat yourself.

97
00:03:04,380 --> 00:03:05,570
And then you step off.

98
00:03:05,810 --> 00:03:06,950
No glass towers.

99
00:03:07,000 --> 00:03:09,540
No mall. Pulau Ubin is described as one of

100
00:03:09,560 --> 00:03:11,610
Singapore's last remaining villages,

101
00:03:11,830 --> 00:03:13,780
a kampong. You rent a bicycle,

102
00:03:13,800 --> 00:03:16,280
for roughly five to fifteen dollars a day,

103
00:03:16,500 --> 00:03:19,400
and you ride. The main attraction is Chek

104
00:03:19,420 --> 00:03:21,950
Jawa, a wetland where several ecosystems

105
00:03:21,970 --> 00:03:24,650
sit side by side inside a single hectare.

106
00:03:24,990 --> 00:03:27,120
It's free to enter, and the tide matters:

107
00:03:27,340 --> 00:03:28,840
the best time is when it's low,

108
00:03:28,930 --> 00:03:31,160
at half a meter or below, because that's

109
00:03:31,180 --> 00:03:32,860
when the different habitats appear.

110
00:03:33,220 --> 00:03:35,120
Around three hundred thousand people visit

111
00:03:35,140 --> 00:03:36,340
the island every year.

112
00:03:36,770 --> 00:03:37,980
Now stand there for a second.

113
00:03:38,370 --> 00:03:41,070
On the main island, Singapore built a bay,

114
00:03:41,300 --> 00:03:43,370
turned it into a lake, banned a stick of

115
00:03:43,390 --> 00:03:45,310
gum, and rolled out a skyline.

116
00:03:46,170 --> 00:03:48,580
Ten minutes by boat, it left a village,

117
00:03:48,600 --> 00:03:50,900
a wetland, and a mangrove alone.

118
00:03:51,390 --> 00:03:53,110
That's the part visitors miss.

119
00:03:53,380 --> 00:03:55,170
The city isn't just something Singapore

120
00:03:55,190 --> 00:03:57,770
built. It's something Singapore chose,

121
00:03:57,800 --> 00:03:58,800
piece by piece.

122
00:03:59,090 --> 00:04:00,570
What to make from the sea.

123
00:04:00,800 --> 00:04:01,780
What to forbid.

124
00:04:02,010 --> 00:04:04,440
What to leave exactly as it was.

125
00:04:05,020 --> 00:04:06,700
So next time you're standing under those

126
00:04:06,720 --> 00:04:08,500
Supertrees, look down.

127
00:04:08,810 --> 00:04:10,600
You're standing on a decision.

128
00:04:11,120 --> 00:04:12,870
If you'd like more places like this,

129
00:04:12,950 --> 00:04:15,050
subscribe, and tell me which country to

130
00:04:15,070 --> 00:04:15,950
visit next.
